 "drivingQuestion": "How does R' Meir read 'kilelat' as divine distress?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rava's reading stands after rejecting Abaye's",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.46b.6",
   "daf": "46b",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "אָמַר רַבִּי מֵאִיר כּוּ׳. מַאי מַשְׁמַע? אָמַר אַבָּיֵי: כְּמַאן דְּאָמַר ״קַל לֵית״. אֲמַר לֵיהּ רָבָא: אִם כֵּן, ״כָּבֵד עָלַי רֹאשִׁי״ ״כָּבֵד עָלַי זְרוֹעִי״ מִיבְּעֵי לֵיהּ! אֶלָּא אָמַר רָבָא: כְּמַאן דְּאָמַר ״קִיל לִי עָלְמָא״.",
   "en": "The mishna teaches that «Rabbi Meir said» that the phrase “For he that is hung is a curse [kilelat] of God” should be understood as follows: When a man suffers in the wake of his sin, the Divine Presence says: I am distressed [kallani] about My head, I am distressed about My arm. The Gemara asks: «From where» is this «inferred?» How does Rabbi Meir understand the word kilelat? «Abaye says:» When a man is hung after he is put to death, God is «like one who said: I am not light [kal leit],» meaning: My head is heavy for Me, My arm is heavy for Me. God is in distress when He has to administer punishment. «Rava said to him: If so, he should have» said explicitly: «My head is heavy for Me, My arm is heavy for Me. Rather, Rava said:» When a man is hung after he is put to death, God is «like one who said: The world is light for me [kil li alma],» meaning: I am light, and therefore the world is heavy for Me, and I am in distress."
  }
 ],
